1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:07,824
Picture the coldest night of a northern winter: frost on
the windows, and the heat pump outside still humming.

2
00:00:07,833 --> 00:00:12,945
Is it still heating the house, or has it
turned into a very expensive space heater?

3
00:00:13,267 --> 00:00:16,483
Meters, not marketing, can settle this.

4
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:24,196
An NH Patch headline put the doubt bluntly: "Taxpayers
On The Hook For Tech That Falls Flat In The Cold".

5
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:33,608
The Mass Save heat pump page answers that "many provide
efficient heat at outdoor temperatures as low as -15 °F".

6
00:00:33,633 --> 00:00:38,361
So does a heat pump give up in the cold,
or carry on as if nothing happened?

7
00:00:39,367 --> 00:00:43,183
Start with the bluntest question:
does the heat keep coming at all?

8
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:50,052
Engineers score heating efficiency as a COP,
and plain electric resistance heat scores 1.

9
00:00:50,067 --> 00:01:04,083
In one evaluation, engineers metered the heat that dozens of ductless mini-splits delivered over
back-to-back winters, and in the later one the units averaged a COP of 1.5 between -10 and 0 °F.

10
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:17,984
That is 50 percent more heat per unit of electricity than resistance heat, which the evaluators called far
superior; in the earlier winter, with deep snow and ice around the outdoor units, the units ran less efficiently.

11
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:26,712
And not only the cold-climate models: the ordinary ones in
that evaluation also ran below 0 °F, just less efficiently.

12
00:01:26,733 --> 00:01:39,261
A newer field test followed prototype cold-climate heat pumps, and leaving out the periods
when backup heat or defrost was running, their median COP between 0 and 5 °F was 1.9.

13
00:01:39,567 --> 00:01:51,351
Those prototypes tested better in the lab, but in the field most still beat 1.75,
the COP the EPA's Energy Star label requires of a cold-climate heat pump at 5 °F.

14
00:01:51,367 --> 00:01:55,711
So the heat keeps coming. But
efficiency is only half of it.

15
00:01:56,233 --> 00:02:03,937
As the channel Technology Connections puts it, "along with
getting less efficient, their actual heat output also drops."

16
00:02:03,967 --> 00:02:14,551
In northern Minnesota, researchers watched one small ductless unit
hold a steady median output from 10 °F all the way down to -15 °F.

17
00:02:14,867 --> 00:02:25,499
Below that it kept running at reduced output, and at -20 °F and colder
it delivered only about 20 percent of what it was rated for at 5 °F.

18
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:32,580
The researchers' verdict was that units like it
should not be used without backup below -15 °F.

19
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:47,984
Efficiency follows the same slope: in the same Minnesota research, outdoor temperature had the biggest effect on
COP, and heat-pump-only cycles managed only around 1.3 at 10 °F, less than half what they reached on mild days.

20
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:49,872
Then there is the frost.

21
00:02:50,200 --> 00:03:01,144
In cold, humid air, frost builds up on the outdoor coil, and to melt it the heat pump runs
in reverse, pulling heat out of the house, which can cool the air coming out of the vents.

22
00:03:01,167 --> 00:03:14,007
In the prototype field test, defrost used a median of 5 to 8 percent of the energy at the temperatures
where it cost most, a small share, though one site spent 16 percent of its time in defrost.

23
00:03:14,033 --> 00:03:17,561
Which brings us to the
real catch: backup heat.

24
00:03:17,567 --> 00:03:25,199
When a heat pump's electric backup strips kick in, Jordan Dearden
warns, "They'll also absolutely destroy your electric bill."

25
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:35,784
In the prototype test, the units ran mostly on the heat pump alone, even at
the lowest temperatures, and backup-only operation was minimal above 0 °F.

26
00:03:36,100 --> 00:03:47,524
But in the coldest range with enough data, below 0 °F, backup elements were on
25 percent of the time, and only some of the 22 sites had enough data that cold.

27
00:03:47,533 --> 00:04:01,741
Some backup use came from the thermostat: backup heat began at around 30 to 35 °F, and the researchers
expect it would start much lower without homeowners changing setpoints, or with smarter thermostat controls.

28
00:04:01,767 --> 00:04:12,255
Some came from faults: two sites fired up higher stages of backup heat during defrost, at
least one did it in normal heating too, and the manufacturer called that a controls problem.

29
00:04:12,267 --> 00:04:28,443
In the Minnesota study's all-electric system, the resistance booster joined in below 30 °F, yet because the heat
pump ran first, the whole system still beat plain resistance heat even at the coldest reading, around -7 °F.

30
00:04:28,467 --> 00:04:40,971
In another field study, one site's compressor would not run below 0 °F at all, even with its lockout
disabled, most likely because of an on-board control setting that cannot be adjusted in the field.

31
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:58,664
Sizing matters too: one study estimated that in Minnesota a high-efficiency heat pump sized for the full heating load cut median bills
by $182 a year, while one sized for the cooling load raised them by $96, because it leaned harder on electric resistance backup.

32
00:04:59,667 --> 00:05:01,539
So what does all this do to the bill?

33
00:05:01,867 --> 00:05:14,443
For the Minnesota homes, which had heated with propane or electricity, the researchers used
performance maps and typical Minnesota weather to estimate cost savings of 30 to 56 percent.

34
00:05:14,467 --> 00:05:30,859
Gas is harder to beat: at the prices of those winters, the mini-splits from the first evaluation cost less to run than
an 80 percent efficient gas system only above 70 °F, which the evaluators said made them no direct replacement for gas.

35
00:05:31,167 --> 00:05:34,743
Against propane, they were
cheaper at every temperature.

36
00:05:34,767 --> 00:05:48,447
Even the measured units listed as cold-climate broke even with gas only above 58 °F; on paper,
the rating curve of a then-current cold-climate model brought it down to between 28 and 40 °F.

37
00:05:48,467 --> 00:06:05,651
A cost model for Toronto and Edmonton that counts equipment as well as energy points the same way: in Toronto, standard heat pumps with gas backup came
out the lowest-cost option for nearly all households, but in Edmonton that held only for some house types, and gas with air conditioning won the rest.

38
00:06:05,967 --> 00:06:13,143
The model's reasons were cheap gas and the cold itself, which
makes heat pumps less efficient and calls on the backup more.

39
00:06:14,167 --> 00:06:17,071
So do heat pumps still work
when it gets really cold?

40
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:29,336
In these field tests most units did: they kept heating below zero, with less efficiency and
less output, and still got more heat from each unit of electricity than resistance heat.

41
00:06:29,667 --> 00:06:37,803
The catch is backup heat, which ran more as it got colder, and more
where a unit was sized for cooling or its controls misbehaved.

42
00:06:37,833 --> 00:06:39,705
And do they still save money?

43
00:06:40,033 --> 00:06:59,185
Against propane or electric heat, the studies here say yes on running costs, though the Minnesota researchers put retrofit paybacks at over 10 years unless the
old system needs replacing; against gas, it turns on the unit, the climate and local prices, and for the mini-splits measured in the first evaluation, gas won.

44
00:06:59,200 --> 00:07:07,192
Each of these field tests followed a limited number of units, so they show
what heat pumps can do in the cold, not what every installation will.

45
00:07:08,200 --> 00:07:17,008
If your winters bite, ask the installer whether the unit is a cold-climate
model, and whether it is sized for your heating load or only for your cooling.

46
00:07:17,333 --> 00:07:24,101
Ask what temperature the backup is set to start at, then keep
the thermostat steady and watch how often the backup comes on.

47
00:07:24,433 --> 00:07:31,465
And if you heat with gas, work out the break-even temperature
at your own prices before you decide what the furnace is for.
